  "global_context_pack": "Core Thesis:\nAn interpretable machine-learning framework combining Extra Trees benchmarking, multi-model SHAP interpretability, and manifold-constrained feasibility sampling establishes an actionable, multi-objective operational basin for a full-scale semiconductor wastewater membrane bioreactor, identifying sludge retention time (SRT) as the dominant fouling driver and raising simultaneous target attainment from 37% to over 61–82%.\n\nGlossary of Terms & Acronyms:\n- MBR: Membrane Bioreactor (submerged PVDF hollow-fibre ultrafiltration with biological degradation)\n- TMP: Transmembrane Pressure (fouling indicator; target <= 0.15 bar)\n- Flow: Permeate Flow rate (production target >= 1.5 m3/min)\n- Level: Membrane Tank Water Level (hydraulic balance target 58–60%)\n- SRT: Sludge Retention Time (operational range 43.3–108.3 days)\n- HRT: Hydraulic Retention Time (operational range 5.0–12.0 hours)\n- MLSS: Mixed Liquor Suspended Solids (operational range 1919–7629 mg/L)\n- F/M: Food-to-Microorganism ratio (operational range 0.012–0.066 day^-1)\n- Glu: Glucose dosing rate (supplemental carbon source, 0.40–1.35 L/min)\n- Air: Membrane aeration scouring rate (operational range 4469–7268 m3/h)\n- C/N: Carbon-to-nitrogen ratio (operational range 4.80–17.50)\n- SHAP: SHapley Additive exPlanations (TreeSHAP and KernelSHAP)\n- SCADA: Supervisory Control and Data Acquisition (4593 continuous hourly records)\n- KDE: Kernel Density Estimation (Gaussian kernel, Scott's rule)\n\nDocument Structure Overview:\n- 1. Introduction: Full-scale semiconductor wastewater challenges, complex organic/chemical loading, and multi-objective optimization objectives.\n- 2. Models and methods: Full-scale system (1,125 m3/h in Asan City), data standardisation, four rigorous validation designs (random 70/30, 20-split, blocked 1–720 h, chronological out-of-time, and independent parallel train B), benchmarking of 16 algorithms across 6 families, multi-model SHAP interpretability, and manifold-constrained feasibility sampling.\n- 3. Results and discussion:\n  - 3.1. Predictive performance: Extra Trees superiority (R2 = 0.988 for TMP, 0.933 for Flow, 0.908 for Level) and stability across all temporal and external validation schemes.\n  - 3.2. Process interpretability: 16-model SHAP consensus proving SRT as the dominant fouling driver across extended aeration; non-monotonic HRT, aeration, and MLSS dynamics.\n  - 3.3. Operational basin: Derivation of the evidence-graded operating window (SRT 43.2–72.2 d, HRT 5.9–6.4 h, MLSS 5580–6138 mg/L, F/M 0.023–0.025 d^-1, aeration 4868–5892 m3/h), achieving 61–82% target attainment, validated on out-of-time data (OR = 3.89).\n  - 3.4. Comparative perspectives: Comparison against single-target and black-box pilot studies, highlighting the value of dual-context interpretability and joint-manifold exploration.\n- 4. Conclusion & Appendix: Methodological transferability to other industrial MBRs and open-source data availability.\n\nOutput Language: English."
